    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This study has been designed to investigate and monitor a groundwater system that was highly contaminated by past military and industrial activities. In this proposal we describe a methodology to assess the effect of a fault and fracture system on the groundwater flow through the turbidite sequence underlying the study area near Los Angeles, California. This formation is composed of alternating sandstone and shale units. Using a process-based methodology we intend to decipher the architecture and the lateral and vertical continuity of the faults and fracture zones. In addition to a quantitative characterization of the fault and fracture system in the outcrop, we will use existing and new wellbore data to determine the 3D geometry of the observed faults and fractures. The knowledge gained will be useful for assessing the role of these structures in the flow of the groundwater in the site.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
